#
# Used while /home/desktop.toml does not exist. The launcher's icon menu
# writes that file (apps, and hidden for the icons taken off), and from then
# on it decides; an entry added here later is appended to its apps at boot
# unless it is in its hidden. Delete it to come back here.
# An entry whose app is not on the machine is skipped, so one list serves
# every board (a board whose storage leaves an app out just shows one less).
# "desktop/launcher" and "desktop/file_manager" are not apps: they open the
# launcher (the menu's Launcher) and the File Manager.
# "linux:<id>" is a Linux PC's application by its desktop file id, shown
# only where it is installed (the Linux PC session; nothing elsewhere);
# "linux:a|b" shows the first of a and b installed (a .deb and a snap).
apps = [
  "desktop/launcher",
  "default/editor",
  "default/shell",
  "desktop/file_manager",
  "/app/game/flappy.app.rb",
  "/app/basic/basic.app.bas",
  "/app/game/breakout/breakout.app.py",
  "/app/tool/nsf_player.app.rb",
  "linux:org.kde.konsole",
  "linux:firefox|firefox_firefox|org.mozilla.firefox",
  "linux:google-chrome|com.google.Chrome",
]
# About /home

/home is yours. Everything you write -- programs, slide decks, your service
list, the settings the machine remembers for you -- lives there, and nothing
under it is ever replaced by a firmware update. It starts empty.

Everything the machine ships with lives outside /home, and an update replaces
it wholesale:

- /usr/share/samples/ruby/       small Ruby program samples
- /usr/share/samples/slides/     PicoRabbit slide decks
- /usr/share/samples/services/   a service list and two service bodies to copy
- /usr/share/services/           the bodies of the services the machine runs
- /usr/share/doc/                this file
- /app/                          the installed apps (tests under /app/test)

To play with a sample, copy it into /home first and edit your copy. That way
the original is still there when you want to start again, and your copy
survives the next update.
